11.3.8 Module VIII — Liquidity Pool and AMM Mechanics (Algorithms 21–23)

Algorithm 21: Constant Product Market Maker (x·y = k)

Purpose: Computes automated market maker (AMM) pricing for tokenized asset pools.

Formulation:[x \cdot y = k, \quad \Delta y = y - \frac{k}{x + \Delta x}, \quad P_{\text{spot}} = \frac{y}{x}]

The constant-product invariant pioneered by Uniswap guarantees continuous liquidity at all price levels, at the cost of impermanent loss for liquidity providers. This mechanism enables atomic settlement of tokenized bond conversions without traditional order-book intermediation.

Complexity: Time (O(1)), Space (O(1)).

Algorithm 22: Concentrated Liquidity Allocation

Purpose: Allocates capital across custom price ranges to improve capital efficiency.

Formulation:[L = \sqrt{x \cdot y}, \quad \Delta x = \Delta \frac{1}{\sqrt{P}} \cdot L, \quad \Delta y = \Delta \sqrt{P} \cdot L]

Capital efficiency gains of 100–400× are achieved relative to full-range constant-product pools by concentrating liquidity within historically observed trading bands.

Complexity: Time (O(1)), Space (O(1)).

Algorithm 23: Impermanent Loss Calculation

Purpose: Measures divergence loss for AMM liquidity providers relative to a hold benchmark.

Formulation:[r_p = \frac{P_t}{P_0}, \quad \text{IL} = \frac{2\sqrt{r_p}}{1 + r_p} - 1]

Impermanent loss is non-negative and symmetric; liquidity providers earn trading fees to compensate for this exposure.

Complexity: Time (O(1)), Space (O(1)).



11.3.9 Module IX — Portfolio Optimization (Algorithms 24–26)

Algorithm 24: Mean-Variance Optimization (Markowitz)

Purpose: Solves for portfolio weights maximizing expected return for a given level of risk.

Formulation:[\min_w \quad \frac{1}{2} w^T \Sigma w - \lambda \mu^T w] [\text{s.t.} \quad \mathbf{1}^T w = 1, \quad w_i \geq 0]

Markowitz (1952) founded modern portfolio theory. The quadratic programming solution yields the efficient frontier of risk-return optimal allocations.

Complexity: Polynomial via interior-point QP solver; typical implementations (O(n^3)).

Algorithm 25: Black-Litterman Expected Returns

Purpose: Blends market equilibrium returns with investor views to produce stable expected return inputs.

Formulation:[\Pi = \delta \Sigma w_{\text{mkt}}, \quad E[R] = \left[(\tau\Sigma)^{-1} + P^T\Omega^{-1}P\right]^{-1}\left[(\tau\Sigma)^{-1}\Pi + P^T\Omega^{-1}Q\right]]

Black & Litterman (1992) addresses the estimation-error sensitivity of classical mean-variance optimization by anchoring to equilibrium returns and using Bayesian updating to incorporate active views.

Complexity: Matrix inversion (O(n^3)).

Algorithm 26: Risk Parity Allocation

Purpose: Allocates capital such that each asset class contributes equally to total portfolio risk.

Formulation:[w_i \propto \frac{1}{\sigma_i \cdot \rho_{i,p}}, \quad \text{Risk Contribution}_i = w_i \frac{(\Sigma w)_i}{\sqrt{w^T\Sigma w}} = \frac{1}{N}]

Risk parity (Bridgewater Associates, Qian 2005) achieves balanced diversification across asset classes without requiring return forecasts, making it robust to estimation error.

Complexity: Iterative solution (O(k \cdot n)).



