11.4.2 Non-Invasive Neurotransmitter Metabolite Sensing Mechanism

The swarm performs continuous, spatially resolved measurement of neurotransmitter metabolites through a three-stage optical sensing cascade:

Stage 1: Aptamer Affinity Binding. Each nanodiamond is functionalized with panel of nucleic acid aptamers selected for high-affinity binding to specific neurotransmitter metabolites:





Dopamine metabolite: Homovanillic acid (HVA), Kd ≈ 2 nM



Serotonin metabolite: 5-Hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA), Kd ≈ 5 nM



Glutamate metabolite: No specific metabolite; direct glutamate sensing via glutamate-binding aptamer, Kd ≈ 100 nM



GABA: Direct aptamer binding, Kd ≈ 50 nM



Norepinephrine metabolite: 3-Methoxy-4-hydroxyphenylglycol (MHPG), Kd ≈ 8 nM

Aptamers undergo conformational change upon metabolite binding, modulating the local charge environment within ~20 nm of the NV center.

Stage 2: NV-Center Optically Detected Magnetic Resonance (ODMR). Nitrogen-vacancy centers in diamond exhibit photoluminescence at 637 nm under 532 nm green excitation. The fluorescence intensity is sensitive to local magnetic fields via Zeeman splitting of spin sublevels. Aptamer conformational change alters the local magnetic environment (via charge reconfiguration and rotational restriction of spin-labeled aptamer termini), producing a measurable shift in the ODMR spectrum that correlates linearly with bound metabolite concentration.

Stage 3: Quorum-Decoded Concentration Mapping — 8 Hz Spatiotemporal Reconstruction. Individual particles perform local measurements; swarm peer-to-peer near-field optical communication aggregates those measurements across ~100 μm quorum domains; and edge computing nodes worn behind the ear (no cranial penetration) reconstruct spatially resolved 3D extracellular concentration maps by tomographic deconvolution. In this revision the entire Stage 3 chain — optical acquisition, quorum consensus, tomographic inversion, and display — runs at a continuous temporal resolution of 8 Hz (125 ms frame period), a four-fold refinement of the earlier 1 Hz specification.

The higher rate is obtained by four-phase interleaved population sampling, not by raising the per-particle readout load. The swarm is partitioned into four phase groups whose readout windows are offset by 31.25 ms; each individual particle therefore reads out at only 2 Hz, while the swarm as a whole delivers a fresh 8 Hz frame. Interleaving holds swarm-aggregate peak excitation power to roughly 2× the 1 Hz all-particle baseline instead of the 8× that unscheduled 8 Hz operation of the full population would require, and it distributes radiative and thermal load across four disjoint particle sub-populations, mitigating photobleaching and local heating simultaneously.

Why 8 Hz is the correct ceiling. The 8 Hz rate places the platform in the same temporal regime as fast-scan cyclic voltammetry (canonical 10 Hz, i.e., 100 ms per scan), the reference method for phasic neurotransmitter measurement, while remaining compatible with a behind-ear, non-penetrating power and telemetry budget. Nyquist analysis bounds the analysable bandwidth at 4 Hz, sufficient to resolve the phasic sub-second neurochemical transients that tonic metabolite averages conceal; the slow clearance kinetics of HVA, 5-HIAA, and MHPG (minutes) are correspondingly over-sampled by more than two orders of magnitude, so the 8 Hz stream degrades gracefully to long-window trend extraction without loss of information.

