D. Quorum consensus estimator

Each particle converts its ODMR readout into a local concentration estimate through its own calibrated gain: [\hat{c}_i(t) = \frac{f_i(t) - f_i^{0}}{g_i}] where (f_i(t)) is the measured ODMR fluorescence contrast, (f_i^{0}) the dark/reference level, and (g_i) the per-particle calibration slope. Particles are then weighted by readout reliability and combined into a domain estimate by a trimmed weighted mean: [w_i = \frac{\mathrm{SNR}_i}{1 + \mathrm{SNR}i}, \qquad m_d(t) = \frac{\sum{i \in \mathcal{D}_d} w_i,\hat{c}i(t)}{\sum{i \in \mathcal{D}_d} w_i}] The upper and lower 10% of (\hat{c}_i(t)) are discarded before weighting (trimmed estimator), giving robustness to aberrant particles. The consensus is Byzantine-tolerant for a degraded fraction (f < 1/3) of the particles in a domain — the same fault model used by the SHA3-256 Merkle-DAG audit chain (Algorithm 29) — so that photobleached, unloaded, or mechanically displaced particles degrade precision rather than corrupting the map. Domains whose consensus SNR falls below the emission gate are flagged as interpolated and filled from neighbouring domains rather than reported as measurement.

E. Streaming tomographic deconvolution

The 3D field is recovered from two measurement sets: the in-swarm primary-domain observations and the transcranial diffuse-optical boundary channels sampled at the temporal-bone window by the behind-ear node. The forward model is linear in concentration, [y_b(t) = \sum_{v} A_{bv},C_v(t) + \varepsilon_b(t)] and is inverted as a streaming state-space problem with a pharmacokinetic random-walk prior on the voxel field: [C(t+1) = F,C(t) + w(t), \quad w \sim \mathcal{N}(0, Q)] [z(t) = H,C(t) + v(t), \quad v \sim \mathcal{N}(0, R)] [\hat{C}(t|t-1) = F,\hat{C}(t-1|t-1), \qquad P(t|t-1) = F,P(t-1|t-1)F^{\top} + Q] [K(t) = P(t|t-1)H^{\top}\left(H,P(t|t-1)H^{\top} + R\right)^{-1}] [\hat{C}(t|t) = \hat{C}(t|t-1) + K(t)\left[z(t) - H,\hat{C}(t|t-1)\right], \qquad P(t|t) = \left(I - K(t)H\right)P(t|t-1)] (H) is highly sparse — each boundary channel intersects only a small voxel neighbourhood — so the per-frame cost is (O(\mathrm{nnz}(H))) rather than (O(n^2)). The recursion is realised in the programmable fabric (PL) of the Zynq UltraScale+ MPSoC, sustaining 64,000 voxels × 4 analytes per 125 ms frame. Regularisation combines a spatial Laplacian smoothness prior, the primary-domain observations as a direct prior, and temporal continuity from (F), which jointly suppress the ill-posedness of the transcranial optical inverse problem.

