11.4.3 Indirect Stimulation via Targeted Ligand Release (No Electrical Current)

Modulation is achieved exclusively through bio-identical ligand release, never through electrical current or voltage application. Two complementary release mechanisms:

Mechanism A: Aptamer-Capped Mesoporous Silica Shell Release. Each 50 nm particle carries an inner mesoporous silica shell loaded with a specific ligand payload (e.g., L-DOPA precursor, selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor micro-doses, GABA receptor positive allosteric modulator fragments). In the absence of triggering, aptamer caps seal the pores. When the swarm consensus algorithm detects a neurochemical state matching a programmed trigger condition (e.g., phasic dopamine drop below threshold, glutamate elevation beyond excitotoxic risk threshold), localized optical signaling uncorks the aptamer caps, releasing femtoliter-scale ligand doses directly into the local synaptic cleft.

Mechanism B: Enzymatic Conversion Modulation. Particles present surface-bound monoamine oxidase (MAO) and catechol-O-methyltransferase (COMT) modulating enzymes that can be optically activated or inhibited, locally tuning neurotransmitter clearance rates without adding exogenous ligand. This provides finer-grained modulation of tonic neurotransmitter levels.

Closed-loop control principle: Release is governed strictly by local metabolite concentration error relative to clinician-programmed setpoints, creating a pharmacokinetic feedback loop analogous to an "artificial astrocyte" that supports endogenous neurotransmitter homeostasis rather than overriding it.



