11.6 MORTAR: Mortality-Optimized Research Triage and Administrative Routing Engine

11.6.1 Purpose and Fairness Guarantees

The MORTAR engine addresses the due-process failures of current Social Security Administration (SSA) disability adjudication queues, in which claimants with terminal conditions or rapidly progressive diseases may wait 2–5 years for hearings, with measurable mortality while waiting.

Core Design Principles:





Anti-Starvation Guarantee: Every claim receives a decision within a bounded time; no claim can be perpetually deferred.



Mortality-Weighted Priority: Claims with elevated mortality risk (stage IV cancer, advanced neurodegenerative disease, severe ME/CFS with cardiac involvement) receive accelerated processing proportional to survival probability.



Locality-Sensitive Hashing: Claims are matched to adjudicators and medical experts with relevant specialization, reducing variance in outcome by medical condition category.



Fairness Auditing: Continuous monitoring for demographic, geographic, and diagnostic-category disparities; automated parity reporting.

Priority Scoring Function:[P_i = w_m \cdot M_i + w_s \cdot S_i + w_f \cdot F_i + w_t \cdot T_i] Where (M_i) is estimated 12-month mortality probability, (S_i) is symptom severity score, (F_i) is financial destitution index, and (T_i) is time already spent in queue. Weights are set via constrained optimization to minimize avoidable mortality subject to throughput constraints.

